-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A — the 0.32 slope is attenuated, and must be reported as an interval
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
log sigma_baseline is itself an estimate. Classical measurement error attenuates
the forward slope toward zero, so 0.3166 is a LOWER BOUND on compression, not the
estimate of it.

RELIABILITY. Two noisy measures of one true volatility are available -- the
random walk's sigma and GARCH's -- and under independent errors their correlation
estimates the reliability:
    corr(log sigma_RW, log sigma_GARCH) = lambda = 0.8410
  Both are fitted on the SAME price history, so their errors are positively
  correlated, which INFLATES lambda and SHRINKS the correction below. The
  estimate is conservative against the compression claim.

                              random_walk      garch
  forward slope                  0.3166        0.2119
  reverse slope                  1.2343        1.0208
  r  /  r^2                 0.6251/0.3908  0.4651/0.2163
  FRISCH BOUNDS            [0.3166, 0.8102] [0.2119, 0.9796]
  attenuation-corrected          0.3765        0.2520
  cluster-robust CIs:
    forward               [0.2609, 0.3811] [0.1375, 0.2933]
    1/reverse             [0.6548, 0.9993] [0.8014, 1.2348]
    corrected             [0.3144, 0.4659] [0.1656, 0.3423]
  WIDEST DEFENSIBLE       [0.2609, 0.9993] [0.1375, 1.2348]
                          entirely below 1  REACHES 1.0

  The Frisch bounds are the honest statement: the forward slope assumes all the
  noise is in sigma_base, 1/reverse assumes it is all in sigma_MAP, and the truth
  lies between. Compression is therefore to be stated as an INTERVAL of roughly
  [0.26, 1.00] against the random walk and [0.14, 1.23] against GARCH, with point
  estimates of 0.38 and 0.25 -- not as "the slope is 0.32".

  CONSEQUENCE, and it weakens the finding: against the random walk compression
  survives errors-in-variables, but only just, the upper bound landing at 0.9993.
  AGAINST GARCH IT DOES NOT SURVIVE -- the interval reaches 1.2348, which is no
  compression at all. Record 5 reported only the random walk's forward slope;
  GARCH's is 0.2119 and its corrected interval covers 1.0. The two baselines do
  not agree, and that was not visible before this correction.

