PART C — provenance of the corrections, including one premise I accepted wrongly
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The premise handed to me and repeated in record 8 Part C -- that measurement
error makes the encompassing test conservative against its own hypothesis -- is
WRONG, and I accepted it before testing it. It considers only error in
log sigma_baseline, which does attenuate that coefficient toward zero. Error in
log sigma_MAP pushes the same coefficient AWAY from zero and dominates here: the
simulated size is 38.6% against a nominal 5%. Record 8 caught this only because
the power run happened to include a size check.

Recording where each correction in this sequence came from, since the record
should show that rather than present a smooth narrative:

  FROM THE OPERATOR
    the A2 critical value must be cluster-robust, not the iid table value
    the baseline control existed and I had not computed it
    the comparison must be paired, not marginal -- overlapping CIs are the weak form
    the sigma cut on |z|>2.5 is selected on the outcome it explains
    a registered negative needs a power statement
    the slope needs errors-in-variables and Frisch bounds
    power before registration, as a rule
    the GARCH restatement in Part A above

  FROM ME, WHILE ACTING ON THOSE
    that S4's naive form cannot fail (z contains sigma_MAP)
    that S4's fixed form cannot fire (null == alternative), found by the
      operator's own power rule
    that the encompassing regression's size is broken, found the same way
    that the mechanism appears unidentifiable, which follows from both

  WRONG AND CORRECTED
    mine:     "the shape departs" from an iid A2 threshold
    mine:     the PIT "tilt" read off one tall histogram bin
    mine:     "cannot rule out a property of the target" with the control uncomputed
    mine:     record 6 labelling the compression null "mechanical"
    mine:     record 8 overstating the GARCH result (Part A above)
    operator: measurement error makes the encompassing test conservative

The pattern worth keeping: every one of my errors was in the direction of a
